    "index": 10,
    "markdown": "Shoes repaired while you wait.\n\nI met him as he was coming out of school.\n\nNow you (come to) mention it, I suppose we *must* have met somewhere before.\n\nYou'll find the way all right once you get to the station.\n\nShe hasn't written since she went away. (Main verb always PERFECT TENSE)\n\n$^{1}$He had no sooner arrived than he demanded a meal.\n\n$^{1}$He had scarcely left the house before we missed the jewels.\n\nThey hadn't gone very far when they met an old man.\n\nFor FUTURE TIME sentences see Exercises 28.17 and 28.21.\n\n\\* \\* \\*\n\n## 58.2\n\n- Join each of the clauses in (A) to the appropriate clause of time or place in (B):\n\n### A\n\n|  1. Come again | as long as is necessary  |\n| --- | --- |\n|  2. Wait | by the time they got back  |\n|  3. He went out again | just as he was ringing the  |\n|  4. They must go home | bell  |\n|  5. There was nothing | as soon as you can  |\n|  left | after he had finished his  |\n|  6. He repaired our shoes | dinner  |\n|  for us | every time I meet her  |\n|  7. I opened the door | since you went to live in  |\n|  8. I haven't heard from | London  |\n|  you | the moment he spoke  |\n|  9. She asks after you | before they get too tired  |\n|  10. I knew who it was | while we waited.  |\n\n### B\n\n## C CONTRAST\n\nCertain conjunctions of place and time are now used to introduce a contrasting clause, very like the clauses of concession (see section J below).\n\nI wanted to go on, whereas my friend wanted to turn back.\n\nNow there is nothing but desert, where there used to be a fertile plain.",
